# Antigravity's CLI, agy, as a kiln harness in a Shards microVM (kiln plan
# 01a1cd8e862e7169a074db3d5fa33f3d, step 26). kiln runs agy in its headless stream-json mode (Kind
# "antigravity", crates/kiln/src/harness/kinds.rs). It comes in as pin.json pins it (pin.sh checks
# each archive's SHA-512 against Google's updater, as Google's installer does) in
# ./antigravity-<arch>/: harness.json and bin/agy. Nothing else installs it, and no sign-in is in
# the image: agy keeps its own in the system's keyring, which a microVM has none of, and step 33
# brings sign-ins from outside the microVM.

# Google's static musl build of agy needs no loader or libc from the base. It checks TLS against the
# system's CA bundle and carries no roots of its own, so any base with CA certificates runs it: this
# default is step 19's, pinned by digest.
ARG BASE=buildpack-deps:trixie-scm@sha256:4f34e1d673fdb65371de9f88bdd32c4e30152fe692b35604aef5c0b3c0e6da10
FROM ${BASE}

# BuildKit sets TARGETARCH in the global scope, so the stage declares it to name each platform's
# harness directory.
ARG TARGETARCH
HARNESS antigravity FROM ./antigravity-${TARGETARCH}

# Its network, every part named (default deny; Shards D236, destinations by name): a network for this
# harness alone, which it joins with no flow to any member, and TCP out to the hosts below alone, by
# name, through the network's boundary (--egress) and the microVM's (EXPOSE ... AS egress). Each name
# resolves for it through the agents' resolver, and no other does. Nothing comes in; no other host,
# port or protocol goes out. 443 is HTTPS's port (RFC 9110 4.2.2). Each host is cited to its vendor's own
# documentation, or, where that names a wildcard or nothing, to the vendor's own source or build, in its
# harness's record's "Hosts by name" (kiln plan 01a1cd8e862e7169a074db3d5fa33f3d, step 18, part 4):
# docs/research/s26-agy-agentfile/README.md, each from Google's own documentation (Antigravity's docs/install.md and
# docs/enterprise.md, Google's OAuth and workforce identity federation docs) and agy 1.5.7's own build. The image serves
# the four sign-ins Antigravity documents, ROOT's call of 2026-11-13 (the person's bar: zero breaks against today), and
# kiln's launch is to keep only the session's own sign-in's hosts (plan step 17):
# - a personal Google account: cloudcode-pa.googleapis.com (its model service), oauth2.googleapis.com (Google's token
#   endpoint);
# - a Gemini API key: generativelanguage.googleapis.com;
# - Gemini Enterprise (Google Cloud SSO, BYOID/WIF) and ADC: cloudcode-pa and oauth2 again, businessaicode,
#   aiplatform and discoveryengine for the license's location (global, or the .us/.eu twins), and
#   sts.googleapis.com, workforce identity federation's token exchange.
# The *.mtls.googleapis.com twins go nowhere: a client uses them only with a device certificate (Google's AIP-4114),
# which no microVM holds. Its logging (play.googleapis.com) and its updater's downloads (storage.googleapis.com; off)
# go nowhere either.
# kiln's MCP isn't granted here, nor in any of kiln's Agentfiles: it reaches the harness over the link
# kiln drives it by, as that link's own `mcp` lane (Shards D254), never over the network.
NETWORK --egress=aiplatform.eu.rep.googleapis.com:443 --egress=aiplatform.googleapis.com:443 --egress=aiplatform.us.rep.googleapis.com:443 --egress=businessaicode.eu.rep.googleapis.com:443 --egress=businessaicode.googleapis.com:443 --egress=businessaicode.us.rep.googleapis.com:443 --egress=cloudcode-pa.googleapis.com:443 --egress=discoveryengine.googleapis.com:443 --egress=eu-discoveryengine.googleapis.com:443 --egress=generativelanguage.googleapis.com:443 --egress=oauth2.googleapis.com:443 --egress=sts.googleapis.com:443 --egress=us-discoveryengine.googleapis.com:443 provider FOR antigravity
EXPOSE --harnesses=antigravity 443/tcp AS egress FOR provider
CONNECT antigravity WITH antigravity ON provider

# Its project is plugged in as it starts, at the path it has on the machine (Shards D235), so kiln
# reads its diffs, HEAD and checkpoints at that same path, and no volume holds it. Its HOME is the
# person's volume, whole: harness.json sets HOME=/home/antigravity, kiln's one rule for every
# harness it ships (step 18). A harness sees its image read-only, with its own /tmp scratch, so a
# HOME that is writable and kept from run to run is a volume, and what the programs it runs keep in
# a home (a .gitconfig, a tool's sign-in) is kept with it. agy keeps its settings, MCP servers and
# conversations under $HOME/.gemini, and no variable of its own moves them.
# HOME in run.env needs a shards at or after 997f4e47e (v0.8.0 and later), where run.env replaces
# init's HOME=/tmp. The volume is the harness's alone, and outside /tmp, which the scratch covers.
VOLUME --target-kind=harness /home/antigravity FOR antigravity
